    how: "Set end-to-end deadlines, then allocate dependency time, attempts, and concurrency within them. Use bounded queues, bounded parallelism, and explicit overload responses. Isolate critical workloads from optional ones and consider tenant fairness. Retry only eligible failures with jitter and a known side-effect contract; assign retry responsibility to a deliberate layer. A circuit breaker limits repeated attempts during failure but does not itself cancel existing work or provide queue capacity. Track rejected work and queue age, not just error counts.",
    steps: ["Slow one optional dependency under isolated load.", "Measure shared pool exhaustion and retry amplification.", "Add separate limits and verify the critical path stays within its budget."],
    flow: "Admission budget -> bounded queue/pool -> dependency deadline -> limited retry or fallback -> observable rejection",
    example: "Illustrative checkout budget: 1.5 seconds end to end.\nInventory may use at most two eligible attempts inside its assigned budget.\nRecommendations use a separate small pool and can be omitted.\nA full queue returns a controlled overload result instead of accumulating unlimited tasks.",
    cheat: ["Retries consume capacity.", "Bound queues and concurrency.", "Isolate optional work.", "Propagate deadlines and cancellation where supported."],
    pitfalls: ["Adding a circuit breaker does not bound memory growth.", "An unlimited Task.WhenAll can overload a healthy dependency."],
    exercise: "Allocate a deadline and concurrency budget for checkout, search, and recommendations. Demonstrate graceful behavior when each dependency is slow or unavailable.",
